{"candidates":[{"content":"Introduce a higher-order attack graph where attack edges are reified as nodes, and the skeptical fixpoint is extended to these attack-nodes. This allows attacks on attacks, enabling a node to be reinstated when all its attackers are themselves attacked. To preserve determinism and polynomial cost, we restrict the order to at most 2 and define a monotone operator on the extended graph. Criticism: this may break the derived property of reinstatement unless the operator is carefully designed; also the graph size grows quadratically, potentially affecting polynomial bounds.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"6ff890a4d3eb1d73b7d5ed91996a87bdbb4be77780452b71692716cbaf1dbfb3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"adb2479eafb0474ad7b3164ad74923dfc256931fbff960d03837d139615e2b79"}],"typicality":0.2},{"content":"Parameterize the skeptical semantics with a rational weight alpha in [0,1] that interpolates between the current skeptical fixed point (alpha=0) and a credulous one (alpha=1), using weighted attack strengths derived from warrant counts. For each fixed alpha, the operator is continuous and deterministic, preserving polynomial cost. Criticizable: requires calibration of attack strengths (cf. SRC_007) and may lose reinstatement for alpha>0; the exact definition of the operator is non-trivial.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"6ff890a4d3eb1d73b7d5ed91996a87bdbb4be77780452b71692716cbaf1dbfb3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"b6d7ac7f6cd8ea654619d86890ee624c6ee9bd8ab490e3dff378ce0be0ed8a3a"}],"typicality":0.3},{"content":"Develop a persistent incremental algorithm that, after each variator edit, updates the skeptical fixpoint by recomputing only the affected subgraph, with a worst-case amortized polynomial bound and a formal proof of correctness. This builds on the caching idea but provides a rigorous complexity analysis and handles deletions and insertions uniformly. Criticism: the dependency tracking may introduce overhead and bugs; memory usage could be high for large graphs.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"f03ef50f69a6f01ee1dbc271065b54b0b320913a46b9f3d49c0c874b46ae2348"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"Create a meta-language for specifying closure rules (e.g., 'any attacker of validity_node attacks the warrant') and automatically compile a set of axioms into attack-edge construction rules, with a proof that any consistent axiom set preserves the guarantees (determinism, polynomial cost, reinstatement, committed-root validity). This systematizes SRC_005. Criticizable: the meta-language may be undecidable; checking consistency of axiom sets could be expensive.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"adb2479eafb0474ad7b3164ad74923dfc256931fbff960d03837d139615e2b79"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"Reframe commitment as case-relative: each committed root is associated with a set of contextual assumptions, and the extension is the set of nodes accepted in at least one context, with a skeptical combination (e.g., intersection of label sets across contexts). This extends SRC_008 into a multi-context framework. Criticizable: may lead to exponential contexts; determinism may be lost unless contexts are totally ordered.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"80255a44b79e939bc4dda1d5411951617558a5952379fbc9dd437c2a31e981b8"}],"typicality":0.5},{"content":"Make warrant-chain provenance first-class in the label assignment: each accepted node is labelled with a set of minimal proof trees derived from committed roots, and the extension is the set of nodes having at least one such proof. This is a proof-theoretic reformulation of the grounded extension. Criticizable: the set of minimal proofs may be exponential; the definition of minimality may be ambiguous.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"ab957e876404fc088730d65b144983031950d2a2b2a66e6e81448f0a045d911c"}],"typicality":0.7}],"simulation_p